course: myths
lesson: 12
title: Cats, dogs and animal 'facts' at home
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Oscar dangles the cat over the banister, grinning: "Relax, cats ALWAYS land on their feet!" It's half true, which is the most dangerous kind of myth.
explain: Cats do have a brilliant twist called the righting reflex that turns them feet-down in the air, but they still get badly hurt in falls, especially from high windows; a clever skill isn't a superpower.
explain: Home is full of pet "facts" like that: wagging means happy, purring means happy, a warm dry nose means ill, a dog's mouth is cleaner than yours, old dogs can't learn new tricks.
explain: Each one picks out one sign and forgets the rest: a dog may wag because it is nervous, and a cat may purr because it is hurt. To understand an animal, look at the whole picture, and ask the owner or a vet.
explain: Some old ideas were never true at all, like cats sucking the breath out of babies, but the worry behind them can still lead to a sensible rule: keep pets and babies supervised.
fact: Cats can twist in the air to land feet first, but they can still be badly hurt in falls, especially from high windows or very short drops.
fact: A dog's mouth is full of germs too, just different ones from ours; neither mouth is "clean".
fact: Dogs wag their tails for many feelings, including nervousness and warning, so look at the whole dog and ask the owner before stroking.
fact: Cats do not suck the breath out of babies; that is an old superstition (a belief with no real reason behind it), but pets and babies should always be supervised for the safety of both.
fact: Many dogs eat grass when they are perfectly healthy, and scientists aren't sure why; if a dog seems unwell, ask a vet.
fact: Cats purr when content, but also when hurt, frightened or giving birth; purring may help them calm down.
fact: Older dogs can learn new tricks with patience and treats; the saying «you can't teach an old dog new tricks» is mostly used about people who don't want to change.
fact: Hamsters run long distances each night and need a big cage with deep bedding and a big wheel.
fact: Rabbits are social animals and usually need a rabbit friend and plenty of space, not a lonely hutch.
fact: A dog's nose temperature changes through the day, so a warm, dry nose is not a reliable sign of illness; eating, drinking and behaviour are better signs.
fact: Advice on a pet-shop label isn't always the best; animal charities and vets give care guides.
exercise: Oscar says cats always land on their feet, so falls are fine. What's true? | They often twist to land feet first, but can still be badly hurt | They never get hurt from any height | They land on their backs most of the time | They can fly a short way to slow down
exercise: What is the cat's twist in the air called? | The righting reflex | The purring reflex | The nine-lives trick | The tail spin
exercise: Grace's dad says a dog's mouth is cleaner than a human's. What's true? | Both are full of germs, just different ones | A dog's mouth has no germs in it at all, ever | A human mouth has no germs, but a dog's does | Dog spit cleans every single wound it ever touches
exercise: Leon rushes at a dog because its tail is wagging. What should he know? | Dogs wag when nervous or warning too, so ask the owner first | A wag always means "come and stroke me now", whatever the dog | Only small dogs wag when happy | A wag means the dog is asleep
exercise: Gran says cats suck the breath out of babies. What's true? | It's an old superstition, though pets and babies should be supervised | It's true for black cats only | It happens if the window is open | It's true, cats do it every night
exercise: Ella says dogs eat grass only when they're ill. What's true? | Many healthy dogs eat grass, and scientists aren't sure why | Grass is poison to them, so they only ever eat it when they're dying | Dogs eat grass to sharpen their teeth | Only puppies ever eat grass
exercise: Alex says a purring cat is always happy. When else might a cat purr? | When it is hurt, frightened or giving birth | Only when it is hungry for milk | Only when it is asleep | Never, because cats purr only when they are happy
exercise: Uncle Joe says old dogs can't learn new tricks. What's true? | Older dogs can learn with patience and treats | Dogs stop learning at age two | Only puppies have brains for tricks | Old dogs slowly forget all of their old tricks too
exercise: The pet-shop label shows a hamster in a tiny cage. What do hamsters really need? | A big cage with deep bedding and a big wheel | A small cage, so they always feel safe and snug | A glass jar with air holes | A cage they share with a cat
exercise: Why do hamsters need so much space? | They run long distances every night | They grow to the size of rabbits | They like to fly about | They need to hide from the sun all day
exercise: Ruby's grandparents keep one rabbit alone in a hutch. What do rabbits usually need? | A rabbit friend and plenty of space | To live all alone so they never fight | A hutch that's about the size of a shoebox | A cat or a dog to keep them company all day
exercise: Noah's grandma feels the dog's warm, dry nose and says he's ill. What's true? | Nose temperature changes through the day and isn't a reliable sign | A warm nose always means a fever | A wet nose means the dog has a cold | Only a cold nose shows illness
exercise: What are better signs that a dog might be unwell? | Changes in eating, drinking and behaviour | How warm and how dry its nose feels to touch | How fast or how slowly its tail is wagging | Whether it has eaten a bit of grass once or twice
exercise: Where can you find good advice on caring for a pet? | A vet or an animal charity guide | Any picture on the front of a pet-shop box | A cartoon or a film about the animal | A saying that your uncle likes to use
exercise: Uncle Joe's saying about old dogs is often really about what? | People who don't want to change | How old dogs' teeth slowly wear out | Dog shows that are on TV at night | The age at which dogs start to go grey
test: "lol watch, I'll drop the cat off the bunk bed, they always land on their feet" What do you say? | Don't, they can still get badly hurt; the twist isn't a superpower | Fine, cats can't be hurt from that height | Drop her from somewhere even higher, so she has more time to turn round | Only kittens get hurt, she's grown up
test: A dog in the park is wagging stiffly, staring, with ears flat. Is it safe to stroke? | Not without asking; a wag with tense body can mean nervous or warning | Yes, any wag means happy | Yes, if you run at it quickly | Only if you stroke its tail
test: Your cat has been hit by a bike and is purring loudly. Your sister says, "Look, she's happy, she's fine!" What's wiser? | Cats purr when hurt too; take her to the vet | She's purring, so nothing's wrong | Purring means she wants milk | Purring means the pain has gone away for good
test: Grandad's old dog Rex is eleven. Your uncle says, "No point teaching him anything now." What would happen if you tried for a week with treats? | Rex could well learn a new trick | Rex would definitely learn nothing | Rex would forget how to sit | Rex would get younger
test: Gran is scared the cat will hurt the new baby and keeps telling the old story. What's a wise reply? | "It's an old superstition, but we'll keep them apart when no one's watching." | "You're being silly, Gran, cats love babies." | "Let the cat sleep in the cot." | "Give the cat away right now."
test: A dog licks a cut on your knee. Your friend says, "Good, dogs' spit is cleaner than ours." Is it? | No, dog mouths are full of germs too; clean the cut properly | Yes, dog spit is a medicine | Yes, it's cleaner than any tap water or cream from the chemist | No, only cat spit is clean
test: Ella's dog Max munches grass on a sunny day, bouncing happily. What does this suggest? | Healthy dogs eat grass too, so it isn't a sure sign of illness | Max is dying and needs hospital | Max thinks he's a cow | Max is out of dog food
test: A pet shop sells a hamster cage the size of a shoebox labelled "perfect home". What's the smart move? | Check a charity guide; hamsters need much more space | Trust the label, the shop knows best | Get an even smaller one so the hamster feels nice and cosy | Keep the hamster in your pocket instead
test: Your grandparents' rabbit lives alone and seems dull. What might help it? | A rabbit friend and more space, with advice from a charity | A carrot every hour | Keeping it in the dark | A mirror in the hutch so it thinks it has a friend there all day
test: Noah's dog has a warm, dry nose but is eating, drinking and playing normally. What's most likely? | He's probably fine; nose temperature changes through the day | He is definitely ill and needs pills from the vet straight away | He is too hot and needs an ice bath | He has lost his sense of smell
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the capacity of the domestic cat to survive falls? | It may turn to land upon its feet, yet remains liable to serious injury | It is invariably uninjured in falls from any height | It lands upon its back in the majority of cases | It is injured only in falls exceeding a full kilometre in height, if ever
exam: Identify the conclusion that may properly be drawn from the observation that a dog's tail is wagging. | None with certainty; the whole demeanour must be considered and the owner consulted | That the animal is invariably content and may be approached | That the animal is asleep | That the animal is suffering from fever
exam: It is given that a cat is observed to purr. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | Purring may accompany contentment, injury, fear or giving birth | Purring occurs exclusively in contented cats that are well fed and warm | Purring indicates that the animal requires milk | Purring is produced solely by kittens
exam: Ascertain the requirements of a domestic rabbit in respect of companionship and space. | It generally requires a rabbit companion and ample space | It requires solitary confinement in a small and enclosed hutch | It requires the company of a cat or dog | It requires neither companionship nor exercise
